---
title: Usage
order: 7
description: Disk space usage — df, du, ncdu, lsblk
tags: [disk, df, du, ncdu, usage, space]
---

Commands for checking disk space consumption at the filesystem, directory, and file level.

@text
df — report filesystem disk space usage.

@code bash
# Show all mounted filesystems (human-readable)
df -h

# Show specific filesystem
df -h /home

# Show filesystem type
df -hT

# Show only local filesystems (exclude tmpfs, devtmpfs, etc.)
df -hl

# Show inode usage instead of block usage
df -i

# Output specific filesystem types
df -t ext4 -t btrfs

# Exclude certain filesystem types
df -x tmpfs -x devtmpfs -h

@text
du — estimate file and directory space usage.

@code bash
# Size of a directory (summary)
du -sh /var/log

# Size of each subdirectory
du -h --max-depth=1 /var

# Same as above (short form)
du -h -d1 /var

# Sort by size (largest last)
du -h -d1 /var | sort -h

# Sort by size (largest first)
du -h -d1 /var | sort -hr

# Top 10 largest directories under current path
du -h -d1 . | sort -hr | head -10

# Size of specific files
du -h *.log

# Total size of multiple paths
du -shc /var/log /var/cache /tmp

# Exclude patterns
du -sh --exclude='*.log' /var

# Show apparent size (actual bytes, not disk blocks)
du -sh --apparent-size /path

# Follow symlinks
du -shL /path

@text
ncdu — interactive ncurses disk usage analyzer.

@code bash
# Install ncdu
sudo pacman -S ncdu

# Scan and browse current directory
ncdu

# Scan a specific directory
ncdu /var

# Scan root (exclude pseudo-filesystems)
sudo ncdu / --exclude /proc --exclude /sys --exclude /dev --exclude /run

# Export scan results to file
ncdu -o scan.json /home

# Browse saved scan
ncdu -f scan.json

# ncdu controls:
#   arrows/j/k   navigate
#   enter/right   open directory
#   d             delete selected (with confirmation)
#   n             sort by name
#   s             sort by size
#   g             toggle graph/percentage/both
#   q             quit

@text
lsblk — disk and partition overview.

@code bash
# Show devices with sizes and mountpoints
lsblk

# Include filesystem type and usage
lsblk -f

# Show size, used, available, use%
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,FSUSED,FSAVAIL,FSUSE%,MOUNTPOINT

# Show only specific device
lsblk /dev/sda

@text
Find large files.

@code bash
# Find files larger than 100 MiB
find / -xdev -type f -size +100M -exec ls -lh {} \; 2>/dev/null

# Find files larger than 1 GiB, sorted by size
find / -xdev -type f -size +1G -printf '%s %p\n' 2>/dev/null | sort -rn | head -20

# Find largest files in home directory
find ~ -type f -printf '%s %p\n' | sort -rn | head -20

# Find directories consuming most space
find / -xdev -type d -exec du -sh {} + 2>/dev/null | sort -hr | head -20

@text
Check for deleted but still open files consuming space.

@code bash
# Files deleted but held open by processes (space not freed)
sudo lsof +L1

# Find large deleted files still consuming space
sudo lsof +L1 | awk '$7 > 1048576 {print $7, $9}'
